<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01GD">Remark <span data-tag="01GD">2.4.4.12</span>.</a> Let $u: S_{\bullet } \hookrightarrow S'_{\bullet }$ be a monomorphism of simplicial sets. Then, for each $m \geq 0$, $u$ induces a monomorphism of sets $E(S,m) \hookrightarrow E(S',m)$ (see Notation <a data-tag="00L9" href="/tag/00L9">2.4.4.9</a>). It follows from Theorem <a data-tag="00LA" href="/tag/00LA">2.4.4.10</a> that if $x$ and $y$ are vertices of $S_{\bullet }$, then the induced map of simplicial sets $\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Path}[S]}(x,y)_{\bullet } \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Path}[S'] }( u(x), u(y) )_{\bullet }$ is a monomorphism. </p>
